provider reasoning: The core issue is that agent-runner is automatically loading the model specified by `default_model` at startup, even when a different model is already loaded and active. This causes unnecessary model switching delays when the provider (like llama-server) needs to unload the current model and load the new one.

The solution is to add a capability to query which model is currently active on the provider—ollama exposes this via `GET /api/ps` while llama-server uses `GET /props`—then check if the active model matches the configured `default_model` before deciding whether to proceed with the switch.

If they match, skip the model-switch cost entirely. If the active model is configured in agent-runner, prefer it over `default_model`. If it's not configured, offer to add it. And if no model is active, fall back to a sensible default (either `default_model` if it exists, or the only configured model if there's just one option).

I need to understand what "active model" means in the context of ollama and llama-server. For ollama, `GET /api/ps` returns loaded models with their details. For llama-server, `GET /props` shows the current model and `GET /v1/models` lists what's loaded. There's a tension here though—the user wants to avoid loading a model when one is already active, but that conflicts with having a configurable default, since the "right" default depends on what prompt the user wants to run.

My proposal: if a provider has a model loaded, make that the effective default and skip switching. If no model is loaded, use the configured `default_model` (or the only available model). But I need to be careful about what "active" means in agent-runner itself. Let me dig into the codebase to see what model state tracking already exists and understand how the provider's loaded state relates to what's configured. The user is asking for a consultation on design, not implementation. I need to verify the current code paths that resolve the default model, understand how the provider tracks the active model, and assess whether the "active model wins" policy is feasible. Let me dig into the direct_transport layer to see if there's already an API for querying the provider's active model, and check the catalog and discovery code to see what's available.

