Let's look at a few more things:
1. How the TUI displays the model selector (Tab/Shift+Tab), and the model-switch notification when sending a prompt — I'll read the rest of tui/mod.rs (the ui_loop part).
2. The rest of spawn_bootstrap / SessionBuilder::start (the budget discovery round-trip happens before the provider connection).
3. I'll also check the context-limit discovery: in the case of llama-server, `/props?model=X` — if the server has a different model loaded, does llama-server serve `/props` for the loaded model? Actually, llama-server with a `--model` specified — `/props` reflects the loaded model. So budget discovery might return the n_ctx of the loaded model, not the model being requested… no, more importantly, when a llama-server is started with a specific model, it only serves that model. If you request a different model name via `/v1/chat/completions`, it returns 400 "model not found" — in that case, the agent-runner retries up to max_attempts, and it will eventually fail with an error. This is the real "model switching cost" — llama-server is single-model, so the server has to be restarted. Ollama, on the other hand, supports multiple models, and the first request to a model that isn't loaded will automatically trigger a load (which takes time).

So "active model" = the model currently loaded into the provider (llama-server is single-model: the one the server was started with; Ollama: the model in the `/api/ps` list). The agent-runner config's `default_model` is a config-level default, and it has nothing to do with what the provider is currently serving. If they don't match (e.g., the user manually started llama-server with model B, while the config's default is A), the runner will first request model A, fail (llama-server) or trigger a long load (Ollama), and only then will it eventually fail, or eventually load A and evict B.

The user's proposed priority order:
1. An explicitly specified model (from the CLI `--model`) probably takes precedence.
2. Otherwise, the currently active model in the provider (queried from the provider).
3. If that isn't configured in agent-runner, prompt the user to configure it.
4. If no active model is loaded: if there's a configured default → use it; if there are multiple configured and no default → defer until the user selects one; if there's exactly one configured → automatically load that one.

